Data: USSPACECOM/18 SDS via Space-Track.org (www.space-track.org),
from its yearly bulk TLE file tle2009.txt.zip (2009).

Excerpt made by tools/iridium_cosmos_data.py (see manifest.json):

- iridium-cosmos-2009.csv.gz: the element sets of Iridium 33 and
  Cosmos 2251 with an epoch from 2009-01-09T16:55:59.806 to
  2009-02-10T16:55:59.806 UTC, and the newest set known at
  2009-02-08T16:55:59.806 UTC of every object passing within
  100 km of Iridium 33 in the 7 days from then,
  as gzipped CelesTrak CSV (the OMM keywords; the TLEs carry no names).

manifest.json lists the bulk file's size and SHA-256, the counts and
the SHA-256 digests of these files.

This is basic space situational awareness data, which the
Space-Track.org user agreement allows to redistribute with the
citation above. It is not covered by keepout's MIT licence.
